      "abstract": "Abstract Taking Adorno\u2019s and Gehlen\u2019s institutional theories as a starting point, this essay explores the distinction between the Institute for Social Research and the so-called Frankfurt School. While often conflated in scholarship, these two entities have unique histories and institutional structures. The establishment of the Institute can be traced through official documents, including Carl Gr\u00fcnberg\u2019s 1924 inaugural speech. Conversely, the Frankfurt School emerged informally, significantly influenced by Adorno\u2019s editorial work for the Zeitschrift f\u00fcr Sozialforschung after he joined Horkheimer in New York. During their time at Columbia University, the dialectical method and writing style became defining features of the Frankfurt School. As the second part of this essay argues, the distinctive mode of writing developed in exile became essential to the School\u2019s institution and legacy.",
