    {
      "paperId": "17b101d6217628c50b8fba8aba7830db163a3087",
      "title": "Convoluting the Dialectical Image",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "M. A. Tata",
          "authorId": "101693370"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2019,
      "abstract": "This volume began as a friendship taking place on a philosophical field at The New School in New York City in the summer of 2017. Here, at the Institute for Critical Social Inquiry, Olivia Guntarik and I met during Michael Taussig\u2019s seminar on the work of Walter Benjamin, aptly titled Thought-Images, Body and Mimesis in Walter Benjamin. This seminar brought together intellectuals, thinkers and writers from all stages of their careers and from all over the world, creating a beautifully reactive mix of minds and personalities from Colombia, Peru, India, Australia, Ethiopia, Europe, the UK and the United States. I\u2019ve always believed in synergy, the way parts create so much more than a predictable whole, and this group was no exception. Together, we all seemed to be forging something I would dare to call Coterie Philosophy, my way of refracting Friedrich Schlegel\u2019s idea of symphilosophy through the lens of Frank O\u2019Hara\u2019s Personism. On our own, who knows what would have happened? Together, we wove our way into each other\u2019s minds, spurring each other on to refine our ideas and maybe even present them to the world publicly. This Special Issue of New Writing is the product of that eventful meeting of minds. Our method was simple: we each focused on engaging with one or two of Walter Benjamin\u2019s Convolutes, the \u2018files\u2019 or even feuilles used to organise the loose, handwritten fragments of Benjamin\u2019s Arcades Project. Our thinking through these Convolutes offers a picture of Benjamin\u2019s obsessions during a time of incredible social and political upheaval. The provocative titles labelling different Convolutes, such as \u2018Prostitutes, Gambling\u2019, \u2018Panorama\u2019, \u2018Fashion\u2019, \u2018The Interior, the Trace\u2019 or \u2018The Fl\u00e2neur\u2019, to name a few, gazed forward enough to capture the creative essence of what it would come to mean to be a writer in the 21 century, putting Benjamin in conversation with a present he in his own way predicted and helped to create. It is for these reasons that we believe this collection will resonate with readers of New Writing as our contributions each in their diverse voices, forms and stylistic repertoires illuminate the dialectic between the writer and the city, non-fiction and fantasy, literature and liberation. Two fellows, Sarah DeMott and Anna Vitale, generously created a stunning schematic mapping all of us via our individual interest in the Convolutes. This productive image structures our collection, as if it were a bit of cheeky dark matter giving shape to a galaxy. The map was its own constellation or star system, and seeing our various positions crystallise in visual form seemed to reveal a deeper structure unifying the astral rhizomes of our thought. This critical image is included as a visual preamble to our work on the subject of the energetic convolutions the Dialectical Image takes as it ripples across history.",
